  "articleBody": "When you’re selling your home, a roof inspection is a big deal, and it’s easy to make a few missteps that can complicate things. Avoiding common mistakes can keep your sale on track and ensure you get the best value for your property. We've seen it all, and a little foresight can go a long way in making the process smooth.\n\nMistake 1: Relying only on a ground-level visual check — how to avoid it: Don't assume you can see everything from the ground. Many issues, like damaged flashing or subtle shingle wear, are only visible up close. A professional inspection includes getting on the roof (safely!) and thoroughly examining all components.\n\nMistake 2: Not providing easy attic access — how to avoid it: If your attic is blocked or cluttered, the inspector can't check for internal signs of leaks or ventilation problems. Make sure the path to the attic is clear so they can assess everything properly.\n\nMistake 3: Failing to document prior repairs — how to avoid it: Any previous repairs you've made to the roof, even minor ones, should have documentation. Keep invoices, photos, and warranty information handy to show a clear history of maintenance and work quality.\n\nMistake 4: Treating normal aging as storm damage — how to avoid it: It's important to distinguish between general wear and tear from age and actual storm-related damage. An experienced inspector can identify the difference, helping you avoid unnecessary claims or negotiations.\n\nMistake 5: Making repairs without retaining invoices or permits — how to avoid it: If you’ve done any significant repairs, ensure you have all documentation, including invoices for materials and labor, and any required permits. This validates the quality and compliance of the work to potential buyers and their lenders.\n\nMistake 6: Ignoring specific buyer/lender certification needs — how to avoid it: Sometimes a buyer's lender or insurer needs a specific 'roof certification.' Don't assume a standard inspection covers this. Confirm any special requirements and ensure your inspection report addresses them directly.\n\nWhat to Do Next\n\nIf you're selling your home in the DFW area or surrounding Texas communities, contact Discounted Roofing Co. for an honest and thorough home seller roof inspection. We'll help you navigate the process without these common pitfalls.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-03"
}